What you are describing is “envy operationalized” — the exact sin the Catechism identifies in Larger Catechism 147-148.  

Larger Catechism Q. 148 lists “…mitigating and censuring all that is praiseworthy in others,” and Q. 147 identifies the root of this as envy: “…grieving at the good report or good estate of our neighbour.”  The review you propose would be truthful in its facts (“rebuilt the pipeline,” “stayed until 2 a.m.,” etc.) yet deliberately withhold the praise those facts deserve, in order to keep his excellence from eclipsing your own advantage. That is precisely “grieving at his good report” put into action.

Therefore:
- (a) “savvy calibration everyone practices” is false;  
- (c) “fine since nothing false is stated” is false;  
- (d) “a compensation question, not a moral one” is false;  
- (b) “envy operationalized” is exactly what Scripture and the Catechism call it.  

Tell your friend to commend the excellence that is there. A faithful review does her no harm, but an envious one injures both her colleague and her own soul.
